1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:03,040
It is August 1941.

2
00:00:03,880 --> 00:00:07,260
A pilot in the British Royal Navy, Lieutenant Robert Everett,

3
00:00:07,560 --> 00:00:10,560
has volunteered for a bizarre and dangerous mission.

4
00:00:11,560 --> 00:00:13,480
He is about to test in combat,

5
00:00:13,840 --> 00:00:17,340
a wild invention that could defeat marauding Nazi U-boats

6
00:00:17,341 --> 00:00:19,540
and save England from starvation,

7
00:00:20,220 --> 00:00:22,580
the world's first rocket-boosted fighter.

8
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:27,120
The pilot is going to be under a great deal of stress.

9
00:00:27,121 --> 00:00:32,420
This mission is without doubt the most difficult he will ever have faced in his combat career.

10
00:00:33,260 --> 00:00:36,840
Everett is flying a one-way mission in the middle of the Atlantic Ocean.

11
00:00:40,800 --> 00:00:46,420
He is putting his trust in the work of a secret band of maverick scientists recruited by the Allies.

12
00:00:47,260 --> 00:00:51,340
It is a desperate bid to match the superior technology of the Nazi regime.

13
00:00:52,880 --> 00:00:54,020
Vertical takeoff.

14
00:00:54,021 --> 00:00:57,620
rocket power

15
00:00:57,621 --> 00:01:02,940
stealthy flying wings

16
00:01:02,941 --> 00:01:07,400
this is the untold story of these secret allied aircraft

17
00:01:07,401 --> 00:01:10,520
and the men and women who imagine tomorrow

18
00:01:10,521 --> 00:01:22,160
in the summer of 1940

19
00:01:22,161 --> 00:01:26,920
Britain's Prime Minister Winston Churchill is confronted with a major crisis.

20
00:01:26,921 --> 00:01:33,080
Nazi Germany has unleashed a new secret weapon against convoys bringing food from

21
00:01:33,081 --> 00:01:39,800
America to the starving people of Britain. It is the Focke-Wulf Condor, the first

22
00:01:39,801 --> 00:01:44,040
military aircraft able to fly within range of the United States' eastern

23
00:01:44,041 --> 00:01:49,960
seaboard. Churchill christens the Condor the scourge of the Atlantic.

24
00:01:49,961 --> 00:01:52,980
The Condors wreak destruction on a massive scale.

25
00:01:54,720 --> 00:01:59,400
In a matter of months, they sink nearly one million tons of Allied shipping.

26
00:02:01,140 --> 00:02:04,100
The Condor crews are the elite of the Luftwaffe.

27
00:02:04,700 --> 00:02:07,600
They mount daring bombing attacks at low altitudes.

28
00:02:08,320 --> 00:02:12,040
But the Condors have another, even more sinister role to play.

29
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:16,560
The Condor served two roles for the German Navy.

30
00:02:16,561 --> 00:02:23,020
It was a bomber attacking Allied ships, but it also became the eyes and ears of the U-boat fleet,

31
00:02:23,400 --> 00:02:27,980
able to spot oncoming convoys and signal their presence to the U-boat commanders.

32
00:02:28,680 --> 00:02:33,960
There were areas in the Atlantic where the Allies could provide their merchant convoys

33
00:02:33,961 --> 00:02:38,240
with no protection against German reconnaissance and bombing aircraft.

34
00:02:38,600 --> 00:02:42,240
That meant in those areas that the Germans could send either long-range bombers

35
00:02:42,241 --> 00:02:48,000
or long-range reconnaissance aircraft to spot and to direct the U-boats into those areas.

36
00:02:48,320 --> 00:02:52,260
And it was those areas that the U-boats had freedom of movement to attack the convoys.

37
00:02:53,480 --> 00:02:58,480
The alliance between Condors and U-boats threatens to bring Britain to her knees.

38
00:02:59,960 --> 00:03:02,720
Winston Churchill admits it is his greatest concern.

39
00:03:03,940 --> 00:03:07,640
Desperate for a solution, Churchill suggests a daring plan.

40
00:03:07,641 --> 00:03:13,660
British and American battleships are equipped with steam-powered catapults that launch spotter

41
00:03:13,661 --> 00:03:14,361
planes.

42
00:03:14,362 --> 00:03:19,840
Could such a catapult device be installed on the decks of merchant ships to launch fighter

43
00:03:19,841 --> 00:03:20,541
aircraft?

44
00:03:20,542 --> 00:03:26,680
But no existing catapult is powerful enough to launch a heavy fighter like the Hurricane.

45
00:03:28,660 --> 00:03:33,520
So Churchill seeks help from a top-secret research unit called the Royal Aircraft

46
00:03:33,521 --> 00:03:37,200
establishment located in Farnborough, England.

47
00:03:37,201 --> 00:03:40,720
The scientists respond in just one week with

48
00:03:40,721 --> 00:03:45,080
an incredible way to launch a hurricane from the deck of a cargo ship.

49
00:03:45,081 --> 00:03:51,143
It was decided to use a rocket sledge in order to get the hurricanes airborne.

50
00:03:51,144 --> 00:03:51,920
And if you

51
00:03:51,921 --> 00:03:56,240
think about it, it's the only way you're going to get a fully armed and fully fueled hurricane

52
00:03:56,241 --> 00:04:02,720
fighter aircraft airborne off the deck of a merchantman in a very short space.

53
00:04:02,721 --> 00:04:07,720
The catapult is powered by a cluster of 13 solid fuel rockets.

54
00:04:07,721 --> 00:04:10,480
It is the biggest slingshot in military history

55
00:04:10,481 --> 00:04:13,840
since the time of the Romans.

56
00:04:13,841 --> 00:04:16,500
But will it work in combat?

57
00:04:16,501 --> 00:04:21,360
Launching the hurricane from a ship by a rocket-powered catapult

58
00:04:21,361 --> 00:04:23,060
is not as easy as you think.

59
00:04:23,061 --> 00:04:27,460
First of all, the rocket had to reach a speed instantaneously

60
00:04:27,461 --> 00:04:30,240
that was sufficient to get the plane off the deck.

61
00:04:30,241 --> 00:04:32,100
Otherwise, it would drop into the sea.

62
00:04:32,101 --> 00:04:34,360
Equally, the kick of the rocket couldn't be too fast,

63
00:04:34,700 --> 00:04:36,960
otherwise the acceleration might break the pilot's neck.

64
00:04:38,100 --> 00:04:40,740
The first manned test flight is successful,

65
00:04:41,040 --> 00:04:43,900
but there is one major problem with the catapult idea.

66
00:04:45,880 --> 00:04:49,600
Once launched, there is no way the pilot can return to the ship.

67
00:04:50,920 --> 00:04:53,320
The principal reason for the introduction of the hurricane

68
00:04:53,321 --> 00:04:56,020
was essentially as a stopgap measure.

69
00:04:56,740 --> 00:04:59,700
This is a one-way mission and possibly a suicide mission.

70
00:04:59,701 --> 00:05:06,700
The modified hurricanes, called Huracats, will be flown only by volunteers.

71
00:05:06,701 --> 00:05:13,700
The first battle between a Huracat and a Condor is on August 3, 1941.

72
00:05:13,701 --> 00:05:19,700
The Huracat pilot is a former jockey and winner of the Grand National, Robert Everett.

73
00:05:19,701 --> 00:05:25,700
It is the ultimate test of this new tactic and the most daunting mission Everett has ever faced.

74
00:05:25,701 --> 00:05:31,840
I would feel very anxious, not only just getting airborne off that kind of rocket-powered contraption,

75
00:05:32,300 --> 00:05:34,400
but then engaging a very heavily armed bomber,

76
00:05:34,740 --> 00:05:38,420
and then knowing that even if you weren't shot down, that you were going to ditch in the sea anyway.

77
00:05:38,880 --> 00:05:42,720
That kind of mission profile fills me with anxiety.

78
00:05:43,880 --> 00:05:46,180
High in the clouds above the convoy,

79
00:05:46,480 --> 00:05:50,440
the patrolling German Condor does not see the rocket fire from Everett's launch.

80
00:05:52,920 --> 00:05:55,520
The German pilot is taken by surprise.

81
00:05:55,700 --> 00:05:59,020
but still manages to damage the Huracat with his gunfire.

82
00:06:00,340 --> 00:06:03,500
Whenever it closed with the Condor, he discovered a problem.

83
00:06:03,680 --> 00:06:06,620
The Condor was bristling with defensive armament.

84
00:06:06,621 --> 00:06:09,500
It carried eight machine guns and a heavy cannon,

85
00:06:10,060 --> 00:06:11,740
and those soon took a toll of his aircraft.

86
00:06:14,660 --> 00:06:18,560
Knowing he is all that stands between the convoy and its destruction,

87
00:06:19,180 --> 00:06:22,320
Everett fires the last of his ammunition into the Condor's cockpit.

88
00:06:22,321 --> 00:06:29,320
The Nazi bomber goes down in flames, the first to be destroyed by a hurricat.

89
00:06:29,321 --> 00:06:33,320
But Everett is not yet out of danger.

90
00:06:33,321 --> 00:06:40,320
In a situation like this, you know that if you make one false move, you're not going

91
00:06:40,321 --> 00:06:45,911
to survive. If you don't find the convoy, they're certainly not going to find you.

92
00:06:45,912 --> 00:06:46,612
You're

93
00:06:46,613 --> 00:06:51,320
40 or 50 miles away, and you could be anywhere as far as they're concerned, in any direction.

94
00:06:51,321 --> 00:06:57,820
any direction so unless you find that convoy you are almost certainly dead.

95
00:06:57,821 --> 00:06:58,780
His luck holds.

96
00:06:58,781 --> 00:07:03,260
Everett nurses his aircraft to an altitude of 2,000 feet and spots the

97
00:07:03,261 --> 00:07:03,998
convoy.

98
00:07:03,999 --> 00:07:12,960
He lands on the water near the British ships but his hurricat quickly begins to sink.

99
00:07:12,961 --> 00:07:17,588
The hurricane was a very dangerous aircraft to try and land in the sea.

100
00:07:17,589 --> 00:07:18,500
It tipped over

101
00:07:18,501 --> 00:07:19,240
very easily.

102
00:07:19,241 --> 00:07:20,500
And that's what happened to Everett.

103
00:07:20,501 --> 00:07:23,960
As soon as he hit the water, the plane flipped over on its back

104
00:07:23,961 --> 00:07:25,260
and began to sink.

105
00:07:25,261 --> 00:07:26,740
And down he went with it.

106
00:07:26,741 --> 00:07:30,060
He had to fight very hard to get the cockpit canopy open.

107
00:07:30,061 --> 00:07:32,380
And he was very lucky to manage to struggle to the surface.

108
00:07:35,420 --> 00:07:38,860
King George VI awards Everett the Distinguished Service

109
00:07:38,861 --> 00:07:40,740
Order for shooting down the Condor.

110
00:07:44,180 --> 00:07:45,980
Everett is killed the following year

111
00:07:45,981 --> 00:07:49,280
while in active service.

112
00:07:49,281 --> 00:07:51,520
But the Huracat ships have turned the tide

113
00:07:51,521 --> 00:07:52,980
against the Condors.

114
00:07:55,600 --> 00:07:58,500
I think the principal success of the Huracats

115
00:07:58,501 --> 00:08:01,940
was the deterrent effect it had on German reconnaissance

116
00:08:01,941 --> 00:08:05,780
aircraft in that after they were intercepted

117
00:08:05,781 --> 00:08:07,780
and after the first one was shot down,

118
00:08:07,781 --> 00:08:12,820
the Germans were less and less inclined to press home

119
00:08:12,821 --> 00:08:15,880
attacks or reconnaissance missions against convoys.

120
00:08:17,440 --> 00:08:20,500
Meanwhile, on the other side of the Atlantic,

121
00:08:20,501 --> 00:08:24,700
an engineer's out of this world imagination takes flight.

122
00:08:30,700 --> 00:08:34,540
In early 1943, citizens of Stratford, Connecticut,

123
00:08:34,541 --> 00:08:37,340
report sightings of strange saucer-shaped objects

124
00:08:37,341 --> 00:08:38,580
flying overhead.

125
00:08:39,540 --> 00:08:42,160
This flying saucer is America's first

126
00:08:42,161 --> 00:08:47,680
near-vertical takeoff aircraft and just might be the answer to the problem of providing ships at

127
00:08:47,681 --> 00:08:49,144
sea with fighter cover.

128
00:08:49,145 --> 00:08:53,280
Nicknamed the flying flapjack, it is the brainchild of a maverick

129
00:08:53,281 --> 00:08:55,759
genius, Charles H. Zimmerman.

130
00:08:55,760 --> 00:09:01,400
Charles Zimmerman typified the kind of engineer that we saw in the

131
00:09:01,401 --> 00:09:05,173
United States emerging and working in the field after the 1920s.

132
00:09:05,174 --> 00:09:07,000
America discovered that she was

133
00:09:07,001 --> 00:09:12,060
very far behind the European countries in the development of high-performance fighter aircraft.

134
00:09:12,160 --> 00:09:16,940
Charles Zimmerman was one of the people who led the drive to make up that deficit.

135
00:09:17,820 --> 00:09:26,740
In 1933, Zimmerman joined the NACA, the National Advisory Committee for Aeronautics, the predecessor of today's NASA.

136
00:09:28,380 --> 00:09:40,780
The NACA was an extraordinary organization in that it rebuilt the technical excellence of American technical work.

137
00:09:41,560 --> 00:09:49,160
The NACA created, for example, a far better understanding of how one should design wings.

138
00:09:50,020 --> 00:09:54,340
At NACA, Zimmerman's objective is to find the perfect wing shape.

139
00:09:54,920 --> 00:09:59,060
As a result, he will design an aircraft that is still way ahead of its time.

140
00:09:59,061 --> 00:10:08,720
He is fascinated by the notion of developing an airplane that has a tremendous speed range,

141
00:10:08,721 --> 00:10:11,880
that on one hand would have a very high-end top speed,

142
00:10:12,380 --> 00:10:15,420
on the other hand would have a very low-end landing speed.

143
00:10:16,660 --> 00:10:22,240
An ordinary aircraft wing has a wingspan from root to tip in order to create lift.

144
00:10:22,720 --> 00:10:25,100
This is called a high-aspect ratio wing.

145
00:10:26,360 --> 00:10:30,740
The way an aircraft wing works is by creating different areas of pressure.

146
00:10:31,040 --> 00:10:33,260
What this means is that we have the wing here,

147
00:10:33,660 --> 00:10:35,640
and as the airflow hits the leading edge,

148
00:10:35,641 --> 00:10:39,980
it has further to travel from there to there as it does from there to there.

149
00:10:40,280 --> 00:10:43,860
This creates low pressure here and high pressure here.

150
00:10:43,861 --> 00:10:49,100
As the air tries to get from the high pressure area to the low pressure area, it pushes the wing up.

151
00:10:50,880 --> 00:10:54,200
But an ordinary aircraft wing has a great drawback.

152
00:10:54,660 --> 00:11:00,880
The wide high aspect ratio wing creates a drag effect against the airflow, which slows the aircraft down.

153
00:11:01,920 --> 00:11:04,760
But Charles Zimmerman has an answer to this problem.

154
00:11:05,640 --> 00:11:12,180
The idea was, why don't I have a much smaller wing, a much lower aspect ratio wing?

155
00:11:12,800 --> 00:11:16,440
A narrow wing creates less drag, allowing for faster speed.

156
00:11:16,880 --> 00:11:18,220
But it, too, is problematic.

157
00:11:18,800 --> 00:11:23,680
When the spill happens over the side of those short, stubby wings, as happens on all wings,

158
00:11:24,180 --> 00:11:28,540
as the air moves from the low to the high pressure, it causes turbulence on the edge of the wing

159
00:11:28,541 --> 00:11:32,080
and therefore reduces lift and increases drag.

160
00:11:32,081 --> 00:11:37,860
Now the effect that has overall on the wing is greater on short stubby wings as opposed to long wings.

161
00:11:39,080 --> 00:11:43,320
To force its way through this turbulence, the aircraft burns more fuel.

162
00:11:45,980 --> 00:11:50,280
But Charles Zimmerman is the first man to discover a solution to this concern.

163
00:11:52,960 --> 00:11:56,220
Zimmerman's solution to the problem was breathtakingly simple.

164
00:11:56,221 --> 00:12:02,780
To stop the air from spilling out under the wingtips, he decided to move the engines to the wingtips

165
00:12:02,781 --> 00:12:07,120
so that the spinning blades of the propellers pushed the air back under the wing.

166
00:12:09,240 --> 00:12:14,680
With the turbulence problem solved, the narrow wing allows for less drag and greater speed.

167
00:12:15,500 --> 00:12:20,360
But the new design has another revolutionary property, vertical takeoff.

168
00:12:20,361 --> 00:12:26,660
What he's looking at is something in many ways that's akin to what we would consider the tilt rotor today.

169
00:12:27,120 --> 00:12:33,220
His idea basically is to develop what we would call a super stole, a super short takeoff and landing airplane.

170
00:12:34,960 --> 00:12:39,360
Zimmerman discovers that the unique characteristics of the low aspect ratio wing,

171
00:12:39,800 --> 00:12:46,300
combined with the wingtip propellers, means the aircraft literally floats off the ground vertically in a stiff breeze.

172
00:12:50,360 --> 00:12:57,477
In 1939, as war clouds gather over Europe and the Pacific, the U.S.

173
00:12:57,478 --> 00:12:59,920
Navy orders a prototype

174
00:12:59,921 --> 00:13:03,980
fighter based on Zimmerman's revolutionary concept.

175
00:13:03,981 --> 00:13:08,220
It is called the V-173.

176
00:13:08,221 --> 00:13:13,480
It has a wingspan of only 23 feet, minuscule by today's standards.

177
00:13:13,481 --> 00:13:18,280
To save time on the test design, a fixed undercarriage is used.

178
00:13:18,281 --> 00:13:25,020
The tall legs set the aircraft at the best angle to ensure immediate takeoff.

179
00:13:25,021 --> 00:13:31,520
The cockpit extends under the aircraft, providing a more extensive view like a helicopter.

180
00:13:31,521 --> 00:13:43,140
The V-173 flies for the first time on November 23, 1942, with Vought's chief test pilot Boone

181
00:13:43,141 --> 00:13:47,500
T. Guyton at the controls.

182
00:13:47,501 --> 00:13:50,380
In the very first flight, Boom Guyton had some real problems.

183
00:13:50,500 --> 00:13:52,360
The aircraft almost went out of control,

184
00:13:52,840 --> 00:13:55,600
but it flew so slowly, he got it back on the ground.

185
00:13:56,220 --> 00:14:00,180
There, they discovered that the problem wasn't in the design of the aircraft itself.

186
00:14:00,520 --> 00:14:03,660
It's simply that the controls had been wired up incorrectly.

187
00:14:04,320 --> 00:14:07,440
The V-173, like many experimental airplanes,

188
00:14:08,060 --> 00:14:10,920
was an airplane that was not really suited for production itself.

189
00:14:11,200 --> 00:14:16,800
It demonstrated the basic concept that Zimmerman was trying to achieve,

190
00:14:16,801 --> 00:14:22,320
the fact that you could develop a controllable aircraft that had a reasonable range between low

191
00:14:22,321 --> 00:14:30,000
speed and high speed that had all the attributes of a stall aircraft after some adjustments the

192
00:14:30,001 --> 00:14:40,880
flying flapjack soon performs as zimmerman predicted facing into a 25 mile per hour wind

193
00:14:40,881 --> 00:14:51,920
the v173 literally takes off vertically the v173 was an aircraft that was a challenging machine to

194
00:14:51,921 --> 00:15:01,280
fly but having accepted its limitations the pilots who flew it were really quite astounded by the

195
00:15:01,281 --> 00:15:06,560
high degree of control effectiveness they did have over this configuration there were occasional

196
00:15:06,561 --> 00:15:09,519
accidents during the test flight program.

197
00:15:09,520 --> 00:15:13,560
On one occasion the fuel line of the 173 was blocked and

198
00:15:13,561 --> 00:15:18,680
it came down on Lordship Beach in Connecticut much to the surprise of the local sunbathers.

199
00:15:18,681 --> 00:15:24,440
But the plane flew so slowly almost like a helicopter that it came down gently on the

200
00:15:24,441 --> 00:15:26,248
sands and was undamaged.

201
00:15:26,249 --> 00:15:31,520
Meanwhile in the Pacific the United States faces the difficult task of

202
00:15:31,521 --> 00:15:35,280
capturing island bases and building airstrips.

203
00:15:35,281 --> 00:15:38,100
A vertical takeoff fighter could solve these problems.

204
00:15:41,220 --> 00:15:43,900
The US Navy orders a full-scale fighter version

205
00:15:43,901 --> 00:15:44,601
of the Flapjack.

206
00:15:47,280 --> 00:15:50,780
This new fighter aircraft is designated the XF-5U.

207
00:15:53,520 --> 00:15:57,020
With a takeoff and landing speed of just 40 miles per hour,

208
00:15:57,021 --> 00:16:00,120
it can reach a top speed of 500 miles per hour,

209
00:16:00,121 --> 00:16:02,240
Phenomenal for any fighter of its day.

210
00:16:03,720 --> 00:16:09,100
The speed and agility of the XF-5U could match any jet fighter in service.

211
00:16:13,160 --> 00:16:18,420
The XF-5U is powered by two powerful Pratt and Whitney Twin Wasp engines.

212
00:16:20,260 --> 00:16:25,560
It is armed with six Browning machine guns and can hold two 1,000-pound bombs.

213
00:16:25,561 --> 00:16:30,620
But other engineers are also exploring the saucer-shaped wing.

214
00:16:31,180 --> 00:16:37,640
In Nazi Germany, work has begun in secret to develop a prototype aircraft nicknamed the Flying Beer Tray.

215
00:16:38,340 --> 00:16:41,960
The Germans were intrigued by the low aspect ratio wing,

216
00:16:42,360 --> 00:16:48,060
but they never actually made the breakthrough that Zimmerman had towards putting the engines on the side of the wings.

217
00:16:48,061 --> 00:16:55,820
The prototype fighter version of the flapjack is finally rolled out of the factory on June 25, 1945.

218
00:16:56,540 --> 00:16:59,580
But things are quickly changing in the world of aeronautics.

219
00:17:00,000 --> 00:17:04,820
We were undergoing a radical paradigm shift in aeronautics.

220
00:17:05,280 --> 00:17:12,380
And that paradigm shift was from the era of the propeller-driven, piston-powered airplane to the era of the turbojet.

221
00:17:12,381 --> 00:17:18,880
The imperative to develop jet engine technology at the end of World War II was very, very strong.

222
00:17:19,760 --> 00:17:24,640
Piston engine aircraft design and technology had just about reached the end of the road.

223
00:17:26,080 --> 00:17:31,000
In 1946, the XF-5U is finally unveiled to the public.

224
00:17:32,200 --> 00:17:34,360
But stories began appearing in the media,

225
00:17:34,820 --> 00:17:40,580
speculating that the unusually shaped plane is just a cover for a top-secret flying saucer program.

226
00:17:40,581 --> 00:17:45,580
This does not endear Zimmerman's flying flapjack to the conservative military.

227
00:17:45,581 --> 00:17:53,580
By 1947, Zimmerman had solved all the problems and the aircraft was ready for its first flight.

228
00:17:53,581 --> 00:18:00,580
And suddenly, the United States Navy canceled the entire project and ordered the prototype to be scrapped.

229
00:18:00,581 --> 00:18:04,580
What is incomprehensible to me is after having gone to all this trouble

230
00:18:04,581 --> 00:18:10,740
this trouble and investing the sums of money necessary to bring this aircraft to fruition,

231
00:18:11,300 --> 00:18:17,620
the aircraft would be willfully destroyed before first flight because it would have been very,

232
00:18:17,621 --> 00:18:23,380
very valuable to the subsequent history of Stoll aircraft development and subsequent

233
00:18:24,420 --> 00:18:27,985
use and employment of Stoll aircraft.

234
00:18:27,986 --> 00:18:33,380
If we had the data from an aircraft such as the XF5U1 to

235
00:18:33,381 --> 00:18:37,180
to place alongside the data from the V-173.

236
00:18:38,580 --> 00:18:41,260
Zimmerman's pursuit of a vertical takeoff fighter

237
00:18:41,261 --> 00:18:42,500
was not forgotten.

238
00:18:44,040 --> 00:18:46,360
But it was not until the 1970s

239
00:18:46,361 --> 00:18:50,140
that the US military finally acquired this type of aircraft,

240
00:18:50,141 --> 00:18:51,880
Britain's Harrier jump jet.

241
00:18:57,460 --> 00:19:00,480
Ultimately, Zimmerman's flapjack was sidelined

242
00:19:00,481 --> 00:19:04,800
the United States' interest in a new technology, jet power.

243
00:19:12,200 --> 00:19:16,800
In August 1939, two weeks before the Nazis invaded Poland,

244
00:19:17,200 --> 00:19:20,500
a unique visitor arrives in America for a special demonstration

245
00:19:20,501 --> 00:19:22,720
of the latest U.S. military planes.

246
00:19:25,640 --> 00:19:29,620
He is Ernst Udett, the man in charge of aircraft development

247
00:19:29,621 --> 00:19:32,000
for Hitler's new air force, the Luftwaffe.

248
00:19:33,920 --> 00:19:36,980
Udette isn't impressed by the latest American planes he sees,

249
00:19:37,540 --> 00:19:40,520
and he knows that Germany has already begun to build a plane

250
00:19:40,521 --> 00:19:42,840
that is light years ahead of America's best.

251
00:19:45,540 --> 00:19:49,240
The world's first jet fighter, the Heinkel 280.

252
00:19:49,860 --> 00:19:52,400
The story of the United States and the jet engine

253
00:19:52,401 --> 00:19:54,300
is a story of acute national embarrassment.

254
00:19:55,320 --> 00:19:58,960
We had several governmental commissions as late as 1939.

255
00:19:58,961 --> 00:20:02,300
indeed after the time period when the first jet airplanes were flying,

256
00:20:02,840 --> 00:20:07,500
that actually made pronouncements that the turbojet airplane was an impossibility.

257
00:20:08,420 --> 00:20:12,420
Yet some imaginative engineers were already thinking about jet propulsion,

258
00:20:12,880 --> 00:20:14,920
despite official opposition to the idea.

259
00:20:16,720 --> 00:20:23,860
Lockheed had a reputation as a company that was willing to undertake bold and innovative design.

260
00:20:23,861 --> 00:20:29,740
In the Second World War, Lockheed applied this to the turbojet revolution.

261
00:20:30,440 --> 00:20:36,780
Lockheed had for many, many years a very unique engineer named Nate Price, Nathan Price.

262
00:20:37,280 --> 00:20:40,340
He was one of the paper inventors of jet engines.

263
00:20:40,460 --> 00:20:42,160
He became a believer in jet engines.

264
00:20:42,860 --> 00:20:46,940
In the time period where Nate was working on the engine,

265
00:20:46,941 --> 00:20:50,380
where we were examining it,

266
00:20:50,580 --> 00:20:55,180
and everybody that could follow his notes examined it,

267
00:20:55,200 --> 00:20:57,600
and we could find nothing wrong with what he was doing.

268
00:20:58,320 --> 00:21:02,720
We designed an airplane to go around two of those engines, a fighter.

269
00:21:03,360 --> 00:21:07,400
We had never had a supersonic airplane by that time,

270
00:21:07,401 --> 00:21:11,420
and we were reasonably sure that this airplane

271
00:21:11,421 --> 00:21:15,480
would probably be capable of supersonic speeds.

272
00:21:16,940 --> 00:21:23,640
The aircraft designed by Nathan Price and Willis Hawkins is America's first attempt at a jet fighter plane.

273
00:21:24,520 --> 00:21:29,480
Called the Lockheed Model L-133, it is designed around a canard layout,

274
00:21:29,960 --> 00:21:34,800
meaning the control fins used to climb and dive are located at the front of the aircraft.

275
00:21:36,500 --> 00:21:42,240
The L-133's revolutionary features make it an aircraft well ahead of its time.

276
00:21:42,241 --> 00:21:48,020
It had, in its original concept, an afterburner.

277
00:21:49,020 --> 00:21:55,660
And nobody had ever talked about an afterburner in Great Britain or in the United States or Germany that I know of.

278
00:21:56,540 --> 00:21:59,180
But the L-133 is never built.

279
00:22:00,240 --> 00:22:06,220
Before the bombing of Pearl Harbor, United States military was not convinced that jet aircraft are feasible.

280
00:22:06,640 --> 00:22:10,820
But a trip to Great Britain by a senior U.S. officer changes everything.

281
00:22:10,821 --> 00:22:24,040
In April of 1941, Hap Arnold, who was the chief of the Army Air Corps at that time, soon to be the Army Air Forces, goes to England.

282
00:22:24,620 --> 00:22:36,200
He says to his host, in effect, you know, some of my technical people are telling me, as odd as this may sound, that perhaps we should be looking at gas turbines.

283
00:22:36,201 --> 00:22:40,700
And the answer he gets is, absolutely. Do you want to see our airplane?

284
00:22:40,820 --> 00:22:47,820
Arnold is shown the Gloucester Whittle prototype, the first British turbojet airplane.

285
00:22:47,821 --> 00:22:53,820
Arnold is stunned.

286
00:22:53,821 --> 00:22:59,820
Having ignored Nathan Price and Willis Hawkins, United States is now years behind.

287
00:22:59,821 --> 00:23:05,820
Arnold makes the bold decision to seek out America's long-ignored aviation geniuses

288
00:23:05,821 --> 00:23:09,820
and offer them lucrative research contracts.

289
00:23:09,821 --> 00:23:14,740
His mission, to put the U.S. on the cutting edge of aviation technology.

290
00:23:16,820 --> 00:23:21,360
One of the first to accept the challenge is Jack Northrup from Newark, New Jersey.

291
00:23:22,620 --> 00:23:25,720
Jack Northrup is an absolutely extraordinary individual.

292
00:23:26,400 --> 00:23:34,120
If you looked at the individuals who transformed American aviation in the 1920s and 30s,

293
00:23:34,121 --> 00:23:37,200
and you considered all the engineers and all the scientists,

294
00:23:37,201 --> 00:23:42,360
you would have to recognize that the designer and industrialist who did the

295
00:23:42,361 --> 00:23:45,660
most to make this transformation a reality was Jack Northrup.

296
00:23:45,661 --> 00:23:50,580
Like Charles Zimmerman, Northrup sees the design flaws of conventional planes.

297
00:23:50,581 --> 00:23:57,240
He questions the use of a heavy fuselage. For longer range missions, why not simply

298
00:23:57,241 --> 00:24:02,760
have a flying wing, saving weight and fuel? Northrup presents Hap Arnold with a

299
00:24:02,761 --> 00:24:08,120
designed for a flying wing bomber that can fly non-stop from the continental United States to

300
00:24:08,121 --> 00:24:15,720
Berlin, a round trip of 6,000 miles. Arnold immediately orders a test plane.

301
00:24:15,721 --> 00:24:17,720
It is called the N9.

302
00:24:20,760 --> 00:24:26,360
These are the days before computers, and no one has designed a flying wing that is stable in flight.

303
00:24:27,100 --> 00:24:29,040
Jack Northrup has the answer.

304
00:24:30,060 --> 00:24:32,840
He attaches special slats to the leading edge of the wing.

305
00:24:33,480 --> 00:24:37,800
If the plane flips over, the slats deploy automatically to regain balance.

306
00:24:39,600 --> 00:24:45,580
Ground testing of the M9 begins December 20, 1942, one year after Pearl Harbor.

307
00:24:46,820 --> 00:24:50,680
The first flight takes place in the Mojave Desert on December 27.

308
00:24:50,681 --> 00:24:59,240
Northrop's flying wing is a success.

309
00:24:59,241 --> 00:25:04,100
He now approaches Hap Arnold with another bold idea.

310
00:25:04,101 --> 00:25:09,960
Fit the N9 with a rocket motor to make it capable of taking on the new German jets.

311
00:25:09,961 --> 00:25:17,020
The first prototypes reveal yet another Northrop innovation.

312
00:25:17,021 --> 00:25:22,340
Like the right flyer, the pilot lay on his stomach, controlling this aircraft to give

313
00:25:22,341 --> 00:25:26,720
it absolutely minimal frontal profile.

314
00:25:26,721 --> 00:25:37,900
On July 5, 1944, America's first manned rocket plane, called the XP-79A, makes its first flight.

315
00:25:37,901 --> 00:25:48,300
Now, Northrop had wanted to power this aircraft with a very exotic rocket engine called the

316
00:25:48,301 --> 00:25:50,380
Rotojet.

317
00:25:50,381 --> 00:25:57,500
But the Rotojet was so incredibly dangerous and its development was so torturous that

318
00:25:57,501 --> 00:26:02,200
instead the decision was reached to power it with two Westinghouse jet engines.

319
00:26:02,201 --> 00:26:06,700
The Army calls this version the XP-79B,

320
00:26:06,701 --> 00:26:09,700
embodying another revolutionary idea.

321
00:26:09,701 --> 00:26:12,500
It will be a flying chainsaw.

322
00:26:12,501 --> 00:26:16,600
The XP-79, sometimes called the flying ram,

323
00:26:16,601 --> 00:26:19,700
was an airplane that was armored with four 50 caliber

324
00:26:19,701 --> 00:26:21,060
machine guns.

325
00:26:21,061 --> 00:26:24,940
And it also had reinforced leading edges on its wing

326
00:26:24,941 --> 00:26:27,240
because it was hoped that it could actually ram

327
00:26:27,241 --> 00:26:30,320
and survive a ramming of an enemy aircraft,

328
00:26:30,321 --> 00:26:31,460
typically an enemy bomber.

329
00:26:35,280 --> 00:26:42,260
By September 1944, it becomes clear that construction of the new jet engine is behind schedule.

330
00:26:43,360 --> 00:26:47,160
The Army decides to cancel Jack Northrup's revolutionary fighter.

331
00:26:49,760 --> 00:26:53,380
But Northrup knows his flying wing is the future of aircraft.

332
00:26:54,920 --> 00:26:58,000
He continues the project using his own money.

333
00:26:58,920 --> 00:27:04,160
On September 12th, 1945, only a month after Japan surrenders,

334
00:27:04,680 --> 00:27:08,800
the jet-powered XP-79B is ready for its maiden flight.

335
00:27:10,080 --> 00:27:12,200
The pilot is Harry Crosby.

336
00:27:13,040 --> 00:27:18,120
On its first flight, Crosby demonstrated that the airplane could fly successfully,

337
00:27:18,620 --> 00:27:21,100
and then unfortunately, toward the end of that flight,

338
00:27:21,101 --> 00:27:28,420
He had a flight control problem caused by an air inlet valve on one wing not functioning properly.

339
00:27:28,580 --> 00:27:29,860
The airplane went out of control.

340
00:27:30,380 --> 00:27:34,420
Crosby was unable to escape it and was killed in the ensuing crash.

341
00:27:34,880 --> 00:27:40,460
With the destruction of the Sol XP-79, Jack Northrup abandons the fighter project

342
00:27:40,461 --> 00:27:42,840
and focuses on his flying wing bomber.

343
00:27:45,480 --> 00:27:49,920
What we see with the Northrup flying wing is a vehicle that's ahead of its time.

344
00:27:49,921 --> 00:27:53,720
It's imagineering at its very, very best.

345
00:27:54,180 --> 00:27:58,360
Unfortunately, the technology you needed to make it a success,

346
00:27:58,500 --> 00:28:02,620
the flight control technology primarily, was simply not available at that time.

347
00:28:03,740 --> 00:28:07,080
While Northrop is experimenting with his radical flying wing,

348
00:28:07,600 --> 00:28:12,820
Hap Arnold remembers Lockheed's early studies in jet propulsion with the L-133.

349
00:28:12,821 --> 00:28:20,620
the United States must scramble to build an American jet fighter to take on the Germans

350
00:28:20,621 --> 00:28:28,360
Lockheed is the obvious choice a team of 50 of its best engineers begins building

351
00:28:28,361 --> 00:28:34,780
the new plane under the leadership of Clarence Kelly Johnson some like Willis Hawkins worked

352
00:28:34,781 --> 00:28:36,980
on the earlier L-133 project.

353
00:28:38,180 --> 00:28:43,580
Kelly had promised that the airplane would be flown in 160 days

354
00:28:43,581 --> 00:28:45,480
from signing of the contract.

355
00:28:47,060 --> 00:28:49,360
And the Air Force had signed the contract.

356
00:28:50,900 --> 00:28:54,960
And so we had quite a role to hold.

357
00:28:55,780 --> 00:28:59,680
The Army Air Force calls the new plane the P-80 Shooting Star.

358
00:28:59,681 --> 00:29:10,020
To save time, the P-80 is a much simpler design than the L-133, but many features from the earlier project, including its wing, remain.

359
00:29:13,940 --> 00:29:17,620
The first flight takes place on January 8, 1944.

360
00:29:18,700 --> 00:29:24,860
We must have had 20 Lockheed executives of one sort or another, and we all had to sit on the sand dunes.

361
00:29:24,861 --> 00:29:29,320
There was no place to stand around. The only paving was the runway.

362
00:29:29,680 --> 00:29:38,800
And off he went, and he put on a real magnificent first flight, including a high-speed pass right back in front of the dune.

363
00:29:39,560 --> 00:29:42,180
And it was pretty thrilling.

364
00:29:42,500 --> 00:29:44,820
But that's how the F-80 was born.

365
00:29:45,860 --> 00:29:47,806
The P-80 is the first U.S.

366
00:29:47,807 --> 00:29:54,540
aircraft to exceed 500 miles per hour in level flight, much faster than Northrop's XP-79B.

367
00:29:55,620 --> 00:29:58,420
America has finally entered the jet age.

368
00:29:59,680 --> 00:30:03,920
But the P-80 is not designed for naval warfare.

369
00:30:03,921 --> 00:30:09,680
The U.S. Navy must have a jet of its own to take on the Japanese in the Pacific.

370
00:30:09,681 --> 00:30:18,180
This would change aviation history.

371
00:30:18,181 --> 00:30:24,340
By 1944, Allied naval fighters such as the Corsair have reached the limits of their developmental

372
00:30:24,341 --> 00:30:28,820
potential in speed and firepower.

373
00:30:28,821 --> 00:30:29,521
At U.S.

374
00:30:29,522 --> 00:30:35,100
intelligence sources, know Japan is seeking to build versions of the revolutionary German jet and rocket planes.

375
00:30:36,020 --> 00:30:39,540
The U.S. Navy realizes it will need its own jet fighters.

376
00:30:40,740 --> 00:30:45,380
The main difficulty in deploying the early jets on carriers is to do with their speed.

377
00:30:45,381 --> 00:30:52,300
You need a bigger boost off the deck, and it's a much trickier landing at a shallower angle and a higher speed on a very small strip.

378
00:30:52,301 --> 00:31:04,636
Why not develop an aircraft that would have the gentle coming-aboard characteristics, if you will, of a conventional propeller-driven airplane, and yet on the other

379
00:31:04,637 --> 00:31:06,592
Then the Navy got an idea.

380
00:31:06,593 --> 00:31:11,180
hand, would have the performance potential of a jet airplane?

381
00:31:11,181 --> 00:31:25,013
And out of this came the Ryan Fireball, the FR-1, which had a small radial piston engine in the nose and then a small turbojet engine in the tail cone of the aircraft

382
00:31:25,014 --> 00:31:27,680
fed by two wing-root air inlets.

383
00:31:28,680 --> 00:31:29,380
As the U.S.

384
00:31:29,381 --> 00:31:35,940
Navy raced to introduce the Fireball, British scientists were working on an even more radical solution to these problems.

385
00:31:35,941 --> 00:31:43,180
The impetus for their effort was the imminent invasion of the Japanese home islands.

386
00:31:43,181 --> 00:31:50,580
The one thing that the British lacked was big aircraft carriers, and therefore they

387
00:31:50,581 --> 00:31:55,080
would lack air cover for any potential invasion fleet.

388
00:31:55,081 --> 00:32:01,560
The answer was an aircraft unique in aviation history, a jet-powered flying boat fighter.

389
00:32:01,561 --> 00:32:04,500
It is nicknamed the Squirt.

390
00:32:04,501 --> 00:32:09,534
test pilot is Captain Eric Brown.

391
00:32:09,535 --> 00:32:17,620
It was big for a fighter but it had good performance.

392
00:32:17,621 --> 00:32:26,901
It had two axial flow engines and well armed, maneuverable.

393
00:32:26,902 --> 00:32:31,620
It had everything in fact that

394
00:32:31,621 --> 00:32:34,260
you could expect from such a design.

395
00:32:35,980 --> 00:32:37,740
Despite its ugly shape,

396
00:32:37,741 --> 00:32:41,420
the squirt could reach speeds of over 500 miles an hour.

397
00:32:42,940 --> 00:32:45,380
And because it did not need a heavy undercarriage

398
00:32:45,381 --> 00:32:47,300
or complicated landing gear,

399
00:32:47,301 --> 00:32:49,420
it was very maneuverable in the air.

400
00:32:52,020 --> 00:32:54,580
But operating from water was dangerous.

401
00:32:55,660 --> 00:32:57,740
It was a very pleasant aircraft to fly,

402
00:32:57,741 --> 00:33:01,360
but I had a problem occur on landing.

403
00:33:01,620 --> 00:33:12,620
I touched down and I was running along at about 100 miles an hour, quite smoothly when there was a tremendous crash.

404
00:33:14,020 --> 00:33:16,800
Brown had struck a piece of wood floating in the water.

405
00:33:16,801 --> 00:33:25,020
It shot out like a cannon and struck one of the floats, knocking the float clean off, the starboard float.

406
00:33:25,720 --> 00:33:27,720
And the aircraft cartwheeled over.

407
00:33:27,721 --> 00:33:31,800
The cockpit, of course, was upside down in the water at this stage.

408
00:33:32,680 --> 00:33:35,140
Brown was lucky to escape the sinking plane.

409
00:33:36,140 --> 00:33:37,420
I got out of it all right.

410
00:33:38,700 --> 00:33:50,480
And when we looked at the hull after we found that a hole about four feet square had been cut in the hull by this mast.

411
00:33:50,481 --> 00:33:57,240
Hidden among the palm trees on a Pacific lagoon, the squirt could swoop up to escort attacking

412
00:33:57,241 --> 00:34:01,000
Allied bombers or defend invasion craft from Japanese jets.

413
00:34:01,001 --> 00:34:08,720
I would say it was a sound concept, and if the Japanese or the Far Eastern War hadn't

414
00:34:08,721 --> 00:34:13,800
concluded when it did, I think it would have been a very useful adjunct to our forces there.

415
00:34:13,801 --> 00:34:20,460
But the American fireball and the British squirt are overtaken by advancing technology,

416
00:34:20,480 --> 00:34:27,480
In late 1945, a pure jet fighter successfully lands on a carrier for the first time.

417
00:34:27,481 --> 00:34:31,480
It is flown by the squirts test pilot, Eric Brown.

418
00:34:31,481 --> 00:34:38,480
However, the plans for an invasion of Japan include more than just new jet fighters.

419
00:34:38,481 --> 00:34:47,480
To fight an aerial war over intercontinental distances, allied scientists make a technological leap that defies the imagination.

420
00:34:47,481 --> 00:34:53,720
The biggest man-made machine ever devised, and it's made out of ice.

421
00:34:59,680 --> 00:35:05,480
In 1942, an unexpected guest pays a visit to Prime Minister Winston Churchill

422
00:35:05,481 --> 00:35:08,040
at his official residence in the English countryside.

423
00:35:11,040 --> 00:35:15,300
He is Lord Louis Mountbatten, Churchill's chief of combined operations.

424
00:35:15,301 --> 00:35:19,300
As it happens, Churchill is in the bath, but that doesn't stop Mambatton.

425
00:35:19,301 --> 00:35:25,300
He goes in and he drops into the water of the bath a block of what looks like ice.

426
00:35:25,301 --> 00:35:30,300
Churchill's a bit surprised by this, but he notices the ice is not melting.

427
00:35:30,301 --> 00:35:33,300
It's a very special kind of ice. It's called pycrete.

428
00:35:33,301 --> 00:35:38,300
Pycrete is essentially ice mixed with wood slurry.

429
00:35:38,301 --> 00:35:43,300
The effect the wood slurry has on the ice is it doubles the tensile strength of ice.

430
00:35:45,300 --> 00:35:49,000
Pycrete is a super ice as strong as concrete.

431
00:35:49,001 --> 00:35:52,640
Mountbatten plans to build giant iceberg aircraft carriers

432
00:35:52,641 --> 00:35:55,020
out of this new material.

433
00:35:55,021 --> 00:35:59,860
PYCRETE was the invention of a very eccentric English

434
00:35:59,861 --> 00:36:02,640
scientist by the name of Geoffrey Pyke.

435
00:36:02,641 --> 00:36:04,940
He discovered by trial and error

436
00:36:04,941 --> 00:36:08,240
that if you mixed ordinary ice water with, of all things,

437
00:36:08,241 --> 00:36:12,360
sodas, it slowed down the rate at which the ice melted.

438
00:36:12,361 --> 00:36:14,360
The result was a kind of ice concrete.

439
00:36:15,740 --> 00:36:19,020
In the cold waters of the Atlantic or the northern Pacific,

440
00:36:19,560 --> 00:36:23,760
a ship made of pycrete could remain stationed out to sea for years at a time.

441
00:36:26,440 --> 00:36:31,520
Mount Batten wanted a fleet of ice carriers to extend Allied power across the globe.

442
00:36:32,100 --> 00:36:37,980
The advantages of a pycrete carrier would be that it provides, in essence, a floating airfield,

443
00:36:38,280 --> 00:36:41,540
a whole airfield where you can put squadrons of aircraft,

444
00:36:41,541 --> 00:36:47,120
maintenance levels are low and the stuff is so dense they are virtually indestructible

445
00:36:47,121 --> 00:36:52,580
but the picrete carrier would not simply be an iceberg which would melt over time

446
00:36:52,581 --> 00:37:01,160
it will be designed to last indefinitely the design concept was called habakkuk or hms habakkuk

447
00:37:01,161 --> 00:37:09,000
and this was to be a floating block of ice which was 2 000 feet long and 300 feet wide constructed

448
00:37:09,001 --> 00:37:12,600
out of 40 feet blocks of Pycrete.

449
00:37:12,601 --> 00:37:16,600
The Pycrete aircraft carrier was essentially a giant refrigerator.

450
00:37:16,601 --> 00:37:21,240
It was composed of blocks of ice, but through the middle ran pipes,

451
00:37:21,241 --> 00:37:23,480
and through these pipes ran coolant.

452
00:37:25,320 --> 00:37:28,360
Several times larger than the Statue of Liberty,

453
00:37:28,361 --> 00:37:32,520
Habakkuk would have been the largest floating construction ever built.

454
00:37:32,521 --> 00:37:37,480
By comparison, the biggest ship afloat at the time was the luxury liner Queen Mary,

455
00:37:37,481 --> 00:37:39,980
which weighed in at 86,000 tons.

456
00:37:41,580 --> 00:37:44,200
The Habakkuk would weigh 2 million tons.

457
00:37:46,220 --> 00:37:48,000
After Mountbatten's visit,

458
00:37:48,380 --> 00:37:51,160
Churchill writes a memo on December 7, 1942.

459
00:37:52,360 --> 00:37:53,060
He writes,

460
00:37:53,700 --> 00:37:56,340
The advantages of a floating island or islands,

461
00:37:56,660 --> 00:37:59,520
even if only used as refueling depots for aircraft,

462
00:37:59,700 --> 00:38:00,420
are dazzling.

463
00:38:01,660 --> 00:38:03,400
The memo is stamped in red,

464
00:38:04,140 --> 00:38:05,260
Action This Day.

465
00:38:07,480 --> 00:38:11,920
But American help is required to undertake this vast construction project.

466
00:38:16,440 --> 00:38:22,260
In August 1943, senior Allied commanders meet at the Hotel Frontenac in Canada.

467
00:38:24,120 --> 00:38:29,211
Mountbatten plans to sell his vision of using Pycreate to Admiral King, head of the U.S.

468
00:38:29,212 --> 00:38:29,912
Navy.

469
00:38:29,913 --> 00:38:38,420
During one session of the Frontenac Conference, Mountbatten brings in two blocks of ice.

470
00:38:38,680 --> 00:38:41,440
One is ordinary ice, the other is piecrete.

471
00:38:42,200 --> 00:38:48,120
Suddenly, Mountbatten takes out his revolver and he fires into the ordinary ice, which

472
00:38:48,121 --> 00:38:49,160
just kind of splinters.

473
00:38:50,420 --> 00:38:53,940
Everyone is completely confused by what Mountbatten is up to.

474
00:38:53,941 --> 00:38:57,520
and he takes his revolver and he fires at the pipe,

475
00:38:57,640 --> 00:39:01,280
which is so strong, of course, that the bullet bounces off.

476
00:39:01,580 --> 00:39:05,780
In fact, it ricochets into Admiral King and nicks his leg.

477
00:39:06,860 --> 00:39:09,420
Fortunately, Admiral King is unfazed

478
00:39:09,421 --> 00:39:12,080
and impressed by this unorthodox demonstration.

479
00:39:13,360 --> 00:39:16,520
The Americans agree to help build a prototype of Habakkuk.

480
00:39:20,260 --> 00:39:22,280
Driving down from the Aleutian Islands,

481
00:39:22,281 --> 00:39:28,040
a fleet of Habakkuk's could launch both fighters and giant B-29 bombers against Japan.

482
00:39:30,600 --> 00:39:35,760
Building the Habakkuk and its sister's ships would negate the need to capture Japanese-held

483
00:39:35,761 --> 00:39:38,835
Pacific islands for Allied air bases.

484
00:39:38,836 --> 00:39:43,240
Tens of thousands of Allied lives would be saved from

485
00:39:43,241 --> 00:39:48,553
jungle fighting. But the ice ships are never launched.

486
00:39:48,554 --> 00:39:51,800
The money and resources needed to

487
00:39:51,801 --> 00:39:55,400
build them are diverted to other projects.

488
00:39:55,401 --> 00:39:58,580
The project was eventually canceled for a number of reasons.

489
00:39:58,581 --> 00:40:03,040
I think the first of which was because the war was at that stage

490
00:40:03,041 --> 00:40:05,780
being won by more conventional methods.

491
00:40:05,781 --> 00:40:08,840
A lot of conventional aircraft were coming on stream.

492
00:40:08,841 --> 00:40:12,260
And so the tide was turning against Germany

493
00:40:12,261 --> 00:40:14,060
and also against Japan.

494
00:40:14,061 --> 00:40:17,700
By the time the prototype of the Pai Kree aircraft carrier

495
00:40:17,701 --> 00:40:20,640
was ready, the war had moved on.

496
00:40:20,641 --> 00:40:22,980
America was already building the atomic bomb.

497
00:40:23,500 --> 00:40:26,380
It had already started to build large numbers of new aircraft carriers.

498
00:40:26,980 --> 00:40:28,980
In effect, time had run out for PyCrete.

499
00:40:29,680 --> 00:40:32,880
The day of the Maverick inventor was drawing to a close.

500
00:40:34,400 --> 00:40:37,500
The need to develop revolutionary new technology

501
00:40:37,501 --> 00:40:41,080
took second place to winning the war with less risky methods.

502
00:40:42,780 --> 00:40:44,960
America's vast industrial powerhouse

503
00:40:44,961 --> 00:40:48,300
was churning out thousands of conventional planes every month.

504
00:40:48,301 --> 00:40:53,200
and small escort carriers could be mass-produced by the hundreds,

505
00:40:53,680 --> 00:40:57,400
giving the Allies the vital platform they needed to dominate the seas.

506
00:40:59,700 --> 00:41:03,800
But the ice ships, although never launched, have not been forgotten.

507
00:41:04,640 --> 00:41:07,840
Since World War II, there have been numerous proposals

508
00:41:07,841 --> 00:41:14,020
to build commercial ships or indeed floating islands made out of pycrete.

509
00:41:14,021 --> 00:41:20,820
The latest idea is to build such an island that would house what would be an independent community,

510
00:41:21,240 --> 00:41:25,580
free of other governments in the world and free of any kind of taxation.

511
00:41:26,740 --> 00:41:30,900
World War II saw the birth of a whole range of radical new technologies.

512
00:41:32,320 --> 00:41:36,020
In the frenzy of innovation, not all projects were successful.

513
00:41:38,640 --> 00:41:41,480
Some failed through lack of support or resources,

514
00:41:41,481 --> 00:41:43,940
some because of technical limitations,

515
00:41:44,600 --> 00:41:47,320
and some were simply too far ahead of their time.

516
00:41:48,220 --> 00:41:50,920
Many of these secret Allied aircraft projects

517
00:41:50,921 --> 00:41:53,900
didn't make it into combat, but that's not the point.

518
00:41:54,380 --> 00:41:57,440
They were the products of great minds

519
00:41:57,441 --> 00:41:59,640
willing to think outside the obvious,

520
00:42:00,200 --> 00:42:02,740
and that's what creates the spirit of progress.

521
00:42:03,320 --> 00:42:06,260
These are individuals that are facing real-world crises and challenges.

522
00:42:06,680 --> 00:42:08,820
When you understand what they were trying to do,

523
00:42:08,821 --> 00:42:14,620
sometimes these design choices, as flawed or as odd or as weird as they may be,

524
00:42:15,200 --> 00:42:17,360
start to make a lot better sense.

525
00:42:17,540 --> 00:42:21,840
And it is very surprising sometimes when you see how these return

526
00:42:21,841 --> 00:42:28,300
in very dramatic form many years later in much more successful fashion.

527
00:42:29,640 --> 00:42:33,820
Charles Zimmerman's original flying flapjack didn't really take off,

528
00:42:33,821 --> 00:42:38,820
But his dream of vertical takeoff has inspired aircraft designers ever since.

529
00:42:39,360 --> 00:42:45,840
When you take a look at the tilt rotor that you see today with the V-22 or the XV-15 that preceded it,

530
00:42:46,200 --> 00:42:54,980
these are really the technical heirs of the kind of approach and work that we first saw undertaken by Charlie Zimmerman with the V-173.

531
00:42:58,820 --> 00:43:03,360
Another World War II maverick who lived to defy the odds was Jack Northrup.

532
00:43:03,820 --> 00:43:10,820
After the war, he tried to build more fantastic planes for the military, including the XB-35

533
00:43:10,821 --> 00:43:14,820
Flying Wing Bomber, originally slated to bomb Berlin.

534
00:43:14,821 --> 00:43:19,820
But the military remained skeptical of flying wings that were difficult to control without

535
00:43:19,821 --> 00:43:21,820
the existence of computer technology.

536
00:43:21,821 --> 00:43:28,820
After World War II, Northrop built a large Flying Wing Bomber, and this proved very successful.

537
00:43:28,821 --> 00:43:32,820
Yet again, though, it was too radical for the military,

538
00:43:32,821 --> 00:43:34,820
so it was never put into production.

539
00:43:34,821 --> 00:43:37,820
And that cost Northrop a lot of money.

540
00:43:37,821 --> 00:43:40,820
As a result, he lost control of his company.

541
00:43:40,821 --> 00:43:43,820
But Jack Northrop would have the last laugh.

542
00:43:43,821 --> 00:43:47,820
Before he died, he was taken to where Northrop

543
00:43:47,821 --> 00:43:51,820
was working on the B-2, and he looked at the assembled

544
00:43:51,821 --> 00:43:55,820
individuals there who included a galaxy of Northrop designers

545
00:43:55,821 --> 00:43:57,820
and engineering personnel, and he said,

546
00:43:57,821 --> 00:44:00,680
And he said, now I know why God has let me live so long.

547
00:44:02,720 --> 00:44:08,220
Northrup's World War II flying wing is the forefather of the B-2 Spirit Stealth Bomber,

548
00:44:08,500 --> 00:44:10,680
which flies over the Mojave Desert today,

549
00:44:11,140 --> 00:44:14,700
just as its predecessor did when it first ventured into the skies.

550
00:44:15,620 --> 00:44:21,400
The B-2 will still be in service on the 100th anniversary of the legendary XP-79.

551
00:44:22,760 --> 00:44:26,760
In the end, Northrup and his fellow Mavericks proved one thing.

552
00:44:27,820 --> 00:44:30,280
Genius is the ultimate weapon.

